#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95bc501d-4124-5179-a51b-315ac85a1b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248757} {:answer/id #uuid "9f69fd84-e05c-5d24-ae8e-9d8f383459c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248843} {:answer/id #uuid "dea80f9c-54f5-5396-b7ef-3e0d29ed1c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249399} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7731aa-f29b-5e06-aa8d-a528db3cff78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{4}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249627} {:answer/id #uuid "ff931226-3ee2-5a37-af2b-7544f41260e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{8}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249660}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248757} :answer-field/id #uuid "75347d01-2014-547c-a5ad-13e10a343907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3b188b68-b90e-5d2a-a750-30bfecf83280" :question/math-academy-id "q-114299" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\int \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21296bbd-9655-58f7-83aa-dba5af0c8f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247716} {:answer/id #uuid "ab51dc5a-a926-51cd-8e7b-df0870f057c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248946} {:answer/id #uuid "e5cbad19-d246-5b98-8980-41bddf12f22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249448} {:answer/id #uuid "e99bc9d8-a545-56d1-baef-23123e5ae336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{6}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249486} {:answer/id #uuid "f8073886-20e6-5ba4-879b-63835b53eb83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247716} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d9a9941-4c05-5900-9e9e-c028b2cee8de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64685827-c31f-5450-80d9-3d65e9e828de" :question/math-academy-id "q-114309" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x+4\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (x+4)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(x+4)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0847d4a6-8406-569f-8fd9-6ee28d2c43d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247496} {:answer/id #uuid "9604a7f9-7867-5499-9942-d113f7cabdb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248760} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bb6439-052e-5829-9382-493c51eccdba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249041} {:answer/id #uuid "f40de5b1-3fd0-5afd-8a8c-b0a694969250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249561} {:answer/id #uuid "f9394f54-b364-5841-85eb-57a59630de67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249598}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247496} :answer-field/id #uuid "5624c3ea-4def-51b8-a6da-e1cf8c816d71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a36b9441-9ddb-586c-961c-0956d2121b3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-114311" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x+3\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (2x+3)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(2x+3)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =-(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c09516b-ffde-5c4c-8dbe-564104924571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{15}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248513} {:answer/id #uuid "a581301a-72c3-5cfb-8ef1-56abefe481a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{25}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248900} {:answer/id #uuid "a5873c57-b416-5bd2-86d9-681f98615c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{25}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248901} {:answer/id #uuid "b120bf14-8c51-5072-89f4-30873910c7df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{25}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249011} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ae0e29-77f1-5100-a586-609d2c797e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{15}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249307}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249011} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ea192b1-985a-504b-a59a-fe45ed09d88a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6d0e94f6-1e93-555f-a728-ca5181d50236" :question/math-academy-id "q-114312" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}{25}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e07b070-117b-50c5-9563-364f2b472707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247691} {:answer/id #uuid "55dba241-a0ad-5dd2-a193-a8f4b6a32a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248201} {:answer/id #uuid "73e6af66-30b4-53c1-9d1f-0d00e757eaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248442} {:answer/id #uuid "80fe39bd-6730-5d90-8ec2-9b32e504d4a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248563} {:answer/id #uuid "fc349b75-7a3e-573b-affc-74cf566b5683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248201} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0fb41b9-ef63-543e-b53e-dffe53d28fbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ea7a186f-be1a-52b9-b47f-3320d955e3e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-114314" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{csc}^{2}⁡x\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{csc}^{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ where $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "771a4680-23b4-5532-bdca-e5e5e51c2e8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}({e}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248472} {:answer/id #uuid "788019d8-4726-57ee-8d3c-f8c472f663ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248481} {:answer/id #uuid "938dda6d-62d5-5932-8236-a8efaf7f79ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248736} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8bee3f-8a28-593e-a9b3-8c87ee384d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}({e}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248941} {:answer/id #uuid "ac64494d-e051-58cd-912e-6906743c182e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}({e}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248941} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cc9c08d-e5e1-5b3f-9652-c2d450c9f225" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ecbdeecb-1f0c-572b-8510-8e18414c1281" :question/math-academy-id "q-114320" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{0}3x{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-2x} & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3x\\cdot {e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}\\right)\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x{e}^{-2x}}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}3x{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{3x{e}^{-2x}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{0}+\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x{e}^{-2x}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{0}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-2x}{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[0{e}^{-2(0)}-(-1){e}^{-2(-1)}]-\\frac{3}{4}[{e}^{-2(0)}-{e}^{-2(-1)}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[0+{e}^{2}]-\\frac{3}{4}[1-{e}^{2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}({e}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e72326f-b37b-556e-9771-d69f461e0b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248129} {:answer/id #uuid "5979c57e-1a61-504f-9748-c153fbd57b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248234} {:answer/id #uuid "82511b39-0f0e-5f20-82f8-c82314f7aca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248578} {:answer/id #uuid "941618c9-aa44-56dd-b179-4d773a5937a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248740} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f13165-476e-58f7-beb6-a83b3ddfdb39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249309}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248234} :answer-field/id #uuid "8956bd65-6e03-5145-875e-cd65f5f6055b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b71aa220-d93a-5e59-b207-8dade0373ae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-114322" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{4}|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{4}|}_{0}^{\\pi }+{\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[-\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )}{4}+\\frac{0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0)}{4}] \\\\  & +\\frac{1}{16}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0)+\\frac{1}{16}(0-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d83e583-d218-50f4-abb7-474c6d01c304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247546} {:answer/id #uuid "3d047995-440b-51a6-837d-6a9c0a9dea33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247961} {:answer/id #uuid "4217347b-9b32-5330-8c51-bcd370f084c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248013} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7776ae-27e1-5fb2-91d8-1d3d11b460fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248680} {:answer/id #uuid "aea67226-a698-5829-a7e0-c9089694e776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248983}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248013} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ff430e6-479c-5fff-ae3c-19a79a5cfe3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "37affd24-3b4f-536c-8927-723157249685" :question/math-academy-id "q-114328" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & +\\frac{1}{9}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0)+\\frac{1}{9}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77d8920c-9eae-5d0e-8971-ca7e7b55533e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2e-3}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248477} {:answer/id #uuid "7b577e2d-1a9b-598e-a5bb-8e6d22918919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{e+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248506} {:answer/id #uuid "82f9258b-b72f-566d-a506-e8c7094ed4bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e+1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248582} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d80631-c8d5-5425-9e65-8029a411c1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249042} {:answer/id #uuid "fb407c98-f1d8-5183-b7e1-e23543be95c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{e+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249609}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249042} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf81a2af-d8b3-5358-9ec2-8ae5505c5bd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e406eb1a-a97b-5da3-8a7a-44bb2a8c8ae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-114329" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}9x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=9x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=9 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x} & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 9x\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =9x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\right)\\cdot 9\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3x{e}^{-3x}+3\\int {e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}9x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3x{e}^{-3x}{|}_{0}^{1/3}+3{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}{e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3x{e}^{-3x}{|}_{0}^{1/3}-{e}^{-3x}{|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =-3[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3(1/3)}-0\\cdot {e}^{-3\\cdot 0}]-[{e}^{-3(1/3)}-{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =-3[\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{3}-0]-[{e}^{-1}-1] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}-\\frac{1}{e}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-2}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "353c38c6-0699-5d31-a3a7-681d96b0021d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247901} {:answer/id #uuid "5507088f-1a8b-5d15-a203-9e83009f5db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248191} {:answer/id #uuid "85d825e8-4b77-570d-9579-44732d982d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248617} {:answer/id #uuid "c26ec3ba-1125-5219-b27d-d1384ee85f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249151} {:answer/id #uuid "d210b2f5-3730-5d88-a77c-6afcdc80ecd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248617} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7cbe6e3-ee8a-56d6-bba3-4dcd2dab931c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0ba270d9-6da5-5830-bec9-26eadbb91904" :question/math-academy-id "q-114330" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 5x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5})-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & +\\frac{1}{5}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =(0-0)+\\frac{1}{5}(-1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3424c38a-0404-5112-b538-b0b51cd67cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247893} {:answer/id #uuid "9f539e40-9fe1-5755-b952-a5dba33c4b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248841} {:answer/id #uuid "abbd5f4c-90d7-5f59-bdc9-b9540e0ed117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{18}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248951} {:answer/id #uuid "c03064a2-589f-5b9e-acac-21e3dde6cf6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249133} {:answer/id #uuid "c273eca8-c57d-58f1-aa76-b2a120fc902e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249152}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247893} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cdda833-7434-58f2-9ede-95f3721b6654" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e837072a-c08e-5c7a-88b6-aa68dac3c245" :question/math-academy-id "q-115123" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf20c8dba07a1c6c2e6e6a95fbe45675301e734c976b8073d67ff4bd2cb21ad6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=6$ and the vertical radius has length $b=9.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "153668da-d780-523a-8e26-48e058715b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247615} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9dc02d-1da3-5526-af85-e13cc50a7c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247968} {:answer/id #uuid "750c26fb-43a5-5a41-8361-58c46e0b0804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248452} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d902f3-35d6-5d0d-ac8b-52610f9c819b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248870} {:answer/id #uuid "a8569992-7633-5a8b-9630-e609ef25ddfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248452} :answer-field/id #uuid "106279dc-5bad-5b89-83c9-6a40e4f9b6f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "522f5175-a019-5a90-95ed-58cc045a0980" :question/math-academy-id "q-115124" :question/problem "![](images/76/764f25a4b18f3f28110c0ce3345402bf183d61cac350832663e221b0de9852a5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18946d18-556b-5c59-afd6-6c11c44a77d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247643} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4292dd-4e78-52b6-8b71-ca0e4809368b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248128} {:answer/id #uuid "5679d9fd-563d-5470-8c16-90f34b8f5565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{64}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248208} {:answer/id #uuid "e447b96f-daf1-55bd-af45-305484c0b5f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249435} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc74f87-91ec-5a2b-ab39-4332409ff087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248208} :answer-field/id #uuid "2043a17f-33cb-56ee-8db3-82cbe5fc75f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "04f04ac8-2bb9-5d12-9232-9b8b1040f8d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-115126" :question/problem "![](images/62/62b62eb01c13ad4eef0367999445402d5a39710b7cfb2df11813b2bfcddb4b9c.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $b=8.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{8}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{64} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f413219-72b6-563c-a2d9-f8afbb029259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248284} {:answer/id #uuid "79b4aef4-9b3f-5d77-ac44-d038f8baee85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248495} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0d68c5-024a-5887-9a04-22c2df46341f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248664} {:answer/id #uuid "aeaf04e9-ff57-5fe8-b96e-fbd8f41c5c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248984} {:answer/id #uuid "c85e571b-6275-59b2-895d-4959a07626cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249206} :answer-field/id #uuid "9207681d-e046-55c2-be9d-c8f1f3489b44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ba6b3820-d361-5a84-a78c-c8c951e27981" :question/math-academy-id "q-115129" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c71a52cc148f8ce4a23a2fb2005757c9d141d3caf05586d8fb5122f591dc7474.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=1$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a537988-fe3f-54c0-9969-840ebacfdc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$16$ and $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247941} {:answer/id #uuid "47dcabf5-4c17-538f-b234-536fc0e79fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$14$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248066} {:answer/id #uuid "84dca1dd-ac85-5c5e-981b-8c8116385a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ and $3.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248603} {:answer/id #uuid "a90d8381-a4e7-5c6c-b202-bb25fb3dee30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ and $\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248927} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe41db8-dff4-598f-8f14-5e33cb612563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ and $2\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249664}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249664} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb465769-f76a-5b09-871d-27ae5361defb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0af912c0-685f-5b27-b24c-c0de6bcff1d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-115132" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=7.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=7,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{7}=2\\sqrt{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab7bfc4-fe0b-5271-b8ca-448cc2f51cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$20$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247805} {:answer/id #uuid "515c4642-4ca5-5554-a9a2-a99042231e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$5$ and $2.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248154} {:answer/id #uuid "5390bb0d-24f6-53b5-94f1-003ff942f21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$50$ and $12.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248176} {:answer/id #uuid "7c004586-c7f0-5ef1-b7fc-ca0dc8582a7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$100$ and $25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248512} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dfbabe-5660-549f-96d1-e202fb8340ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249580}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247805} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ac28089-ed40-5056-81d0-446e067aefea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "41a42db1-4c6e-506f-8c7d-8db6141e0014" :question/math-academy-id "q-115135" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=100$ and ${b}^{2}=25.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=100,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{100}=10.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=10,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=5.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 10=20,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 5=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256656 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7542f2f6-b892-5626-afb8-db34cd70e0c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254709} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c6ea31-331c-56c0-bd88-8b436b2b7eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255170} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ea6601-5749-54c9-ba36-44e09b3b0dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{t}^{3}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255681} {:answer/id #uuid "e7df80cd-42a0-564e-887c-f1473180be67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255709} {:answer/id #uuid "f5478718-e38f-51df-ba4d-0a7c66983c0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255170} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffffbcf7-2290-5771-97a7-2e3fca38c6aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c37df0ad-2178-59ae-82b8-7b50bbb1ab58" :question/math-academy-id "q-122530" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $3x=y$ traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(3,9).$ Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n3x=y\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{3},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 9\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 9$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{3}\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=9{x}^{2}y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9{\\left(\\frac{t}{3}\\right)}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =9\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{9}\\right)(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d0041b6-b7a6-544b-a5a1-4601e83eb423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253911} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba4acff-ed4d-5004-82bf-b89074e988ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{e}^{2e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254189} {:answer/id #uuid "76b7eee7-6a99-5701-a4bd-ca2f9ad5ab1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{e}^{2e}t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254718} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd7ab28-aada-5ff7-ae14-4e7c1b105102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255041} {:answer/id #uuid "f299ef39-a3f8-5921-9d4d-66255a05f5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253911} :answer-field/id #uuid "98b25ddd-1b34-5ba6-b53a-dc771bfba0c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2f457a6-0776-5f23-8cdb-bf0ad17c2cda" :question/math-academy-id "q-122534" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6cabe5598368b4f26ccca515d4f4011100e0bc410b72d919db9681b3145814fb.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,{e}^{2}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)={e}^{x}{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}){t}^{2} \\\\  & =(t){t}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256667 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47302108-d687-5b9f-8466-13bda0aa9a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254273} {:answer/id #uuid "635dfe49-19a8-5b28-a7d6-0119af1c3aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254563} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7f9994-8ead-51fc-a0bc-e35e1c013a56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254628} {:answer/id #uuid "843a82b2-282f-5e4f-91da-3c42e53d7862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6(36-\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254846} {:answer/id #uuid "8acd42aa-8188-53b4-8b19-2defe96db9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5(25-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254894}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254894} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0aed9aa-ec0d-5834-81f4-f0a6213310d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c9c9eb7b-4691-5649-a35d-df87aec5b233" :question/math-academy-id "q-122574" :question/problem "![](images/02/02582b9146af7bfa73ade5c4bd26ec7102650f324fede161f98482153730e868.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x={y}^{2}$ from $(1,1)$ to $(6,\\sqrt{6}),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4{t}^{2}+1,$ $\\text{d}u=8t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =12{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{25}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =125-5\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =5(25-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "244cbd35-4706-5f3b-a48a-e1061c8c3156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253996} {:answer/id #uuid "2dad97c8-3c56-5c9a-a8c2-77eadd3dfd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254080} {:answer/id #uuid "378b4c07-594e-5889-86f8-ae72d53a2f38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254151} {:answer/id #uuid "941292e1-b3c5-55ef-a3f3-007f8b0b0228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254972} {:answer/id #uuid "f98e4257-7cc8-5955-bed8-55177dfe7d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255855}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255855} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3046664-9e6f-53e3-9c0f-658dd83eef2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7198c6d2-e4a6-5178-afaa-7f9ff2209737" :question/math-academy-id "q-122593" :question/problem "![](images/46/46f2f23c1cc2cb2caacd3703665af673a52a7e876665c7ba9420ae72138e1068.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ from $(2,1)$ to $(1,2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+4}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=x{y}^{6},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2}{t}\\cdot {t}^{6} \\\\  & =2{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{t}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{4}+4,$ $\\text{d}u=4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{20}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{40\\sqrt{5}-5\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0820aec6-52e0-5e49-a59a-3bbb2eaf3087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253728} {:answer/id #uuid "28546720-1acd-59ba-82be-bb4ffaf85ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "yz\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,i+xz\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,j+xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254032} {:answer/id #uuid "707b941f-3995-54da-8e24-b82c909648d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254666} {:answer/id #uuid "86a81cb8-c83d-53e2-85a7-df445fd4a517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2yz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2xz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,j+\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254861} {:answer/id #uuid "d766fd6b-8bc8-553e-b31b-478e1a2b8eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,i+2y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,j+2z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255575}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254666} :answer-field/id #uuid "96de8d74-918e-533e-ac2c-2823c4173a35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ec9f81d0-f152-5c1c-b7c8-fd08eb67e7c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122596" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6a5e9d-527e-52ec-ba7f-90eb833346f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254187} {:answer/id #uuid "4210211b-29fa-55e2-a0b7-52ff66e974ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254233} {:answer/id #uuid "445bfc7e-e291-543e-855e-38c46c84f040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254250} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e3b28e-4aac-520e-a4d4-79a1adb3c2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255541} {:answer/id #uuid "f337cd0f-4b55-5597-858a-91b7156059ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255798}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255798} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a0aba96-bf2c-522a-b26d-f17993fb8c81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bcca6806-23f0-5850-a949-44a533788116" :question/math-academy-id "q-122607" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0560a0e5-3c64-5c20-ba01-0f4c2befe53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y)=2\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253708} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1acef0-096e-5556-9f93-be14a82e5b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253907} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc6c128-ebca-5a8b-9698-2fa7cca63d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254190} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0fc214-fc5a-5e41-9871-649ee0d5d24b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y)=i+x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254495} {:answer/id #uuid "a5376f4d-7b12-543b-a551-ae03955027c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y)=i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254495} :answer-field/id #uuid "948987f7-66fc-57d2-8b1e-f9f08b7df17e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d79ce833-b5df-5f7a-a69b-3af96b3bd56d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122629" :question/problem "![](images/34/34aca579b2890586365298c764d051f57d4c80977ee975caad505c923343f90d.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=-i+x\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=i+2\\,j,\\,H(x,y)=2\\,i-j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y)=i+x\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0feaf718-9c43-5258-8ff7-15b33841980d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y)=-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253812} {:answer/id #uuid "240ad4bf-ad85-5774-8e63-d0fe2d0c5637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y)=-i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253994} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff572d8-afc6-5dba-b636-ebe15c35810d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254365} {:answer/id #uuid "505958de-9427-5e52-8d6c-57dee92e3f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254369} {:answer/id #uuid "5a33bd05-aafe-5ca0-8291-65d2e919e162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254462}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254369} :answer-field/id #uuid "31154f67-e955-5021-90d3-73fe1888a1b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "01c701e2-d5c4-5318-a60e-f5d847761bce" :question/math-academy-id "q-122631" :question/problem "![](images/77/77f88ec1dfbd85b4609800484963d2480e68009eb4ac1f6c9fb620914633ff2a.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=-i+2\\,j,\\,H(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y)=-2\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y)=-y\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba98b8c-2116-50e2-b2de-6aae7dbae22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y)=-i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254058} {:answer/id #uuid "735ac856-ee4b-5c8a-9daf-474103fa33a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y)=x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254692} {:answer/id #uuid "a2214b75-4ff9-5986-b2e2-09f3f7eea2eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255100} {:answer/id #uuid "e51e621f-adf6-56d4-ba3a-23552d243dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y)=5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255694} {:answer/id #uuid "fce86be7-59a1-5b1f-98d2-ae97d81427f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254692} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1d0012-9007-5d62-a389-7be8930ea3d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b5e48f80-38d5-59fb-be5e-a4668cd85716" :question/math-academy-id "q-122632" :question/problem "![](images/a0/a032eb49f78ce7bd54de6969f030e4316f2bd3028280f73991af3fe45521733c.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=i+j,\\,K(x,y)=-i-2\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=5\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y)=x\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1594a1bc-f5fe-5f70-9851-0a40cf220726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y)=y\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253858} {:answer/id #uuid "2a58e0ad-829d-534a-8952-ebde58ad8036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=x\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254047} {:answer/id #uuid "806ab92f-6629-5c21-bfaa-aed813f56f31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y)=y\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254806} {:answer/id #uuid "a3040035-3b3f-530d-ab79-811b4208c92b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y)=-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255108} {:answer/id #uuid "c489f169-2513-54a3-8d41-2efa377862b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255426}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254806} :answer-field/id #uuid "7217108e-c492-5bb5-ac37-d69d40646a57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "de025ac4-8073-535b-a254-6c2938769096" :question/math-academy-id "q-122633" :question/problem "![](images/25/251e49513353092b4f9a16f54bb14fbaa1afe1a14e5fecf5b3ce54905fed474d.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $j$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y)=y\\,i+j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $i$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=x\\,i,\\,K(x,y)=-i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y)=y\\,i.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256683 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76b802a1-bd57-507e-9d5f-2fc8163a8ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254720} {:answer/id #uuid "a032e651-0cf8-5329-91d1-1c3f7ceb5a0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255082} {:answer/id #uuid "a7435040-9bd5-51fd-be96-decf4409f227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255149} {:answer/id #uuid "c0557434-e79c-58c5-a3d7-f32cbf4b4359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y)=-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255381} {:answer/id #uuid "e02a5494-bc39-5aa0-84ae-eb5ee949e1e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y)=j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255663}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254720} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff28f4c7-b721-56cb-a6ef-a2b3a670e104" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4eb6b577-c2ea-57ca-93b4-a6dc4a523e65" :question/math-academy-id "q-122634" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d13f8c3fdea65c5f05df12585aec7e18564bdbe390ebb895b8b2d01dda74708a.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $j$ component) is zero for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=j,\\,F(x,y)=-j\n$$\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is positive for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y)=-i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y)=i.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1841502c-c704-5a4b-94ce-aa056d8b2d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y)=-3\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253879} {:answer/id #uuid "726b2edc-7b83-5828-8edf-c66b8e7d2498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y)=2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254684} {:answer/id #uuid "a86f4809-c346-50e5-bed8-9083aa709ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255154} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5823e1-ac01-5c74-ae2d-cf1e431ff2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=4\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255330} {:answer/id #uuid "f25fec05-4300-51c1-8fa4-4555e55ebdab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y)=-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255789}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255789} :answer-field/id #uuid "3803374d-ed66-5409-8623-1564a0c2ca2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b6a55635-2f53-567e-bc46-3d92630e23fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-122637" :question/problem "![](images/72/721252c30370d98fe776ca00e43fef9c95a5a074140dfbdf2a00be19a66b4a1e.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=4\\,i,\\,K(x,y)=-3\\,i\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $j$ component) is negative for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=2\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y)=-5\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f6c3873-4d1d-574a-a9c2-8b7237391726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i+x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253944} {:answer/id #uuid "77a79fac-2c83-5559-8e88-df7821819c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254728} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc3b387-dc15-5110-8f89-a3ced8863a54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254776} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b92edb-bdc2-5822-8401-8722052a64cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255150} {:answer/id #uuid "efac23a6-983f-5fcf-b670-3454bacfa6f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=x\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255764}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255150} :answer-field/id #uuid "41312e99-823c-57ca-bbea-af3327d42ce6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b83bfdca-4e4b-517b-aad8-aed17555d2d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122651" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7cb4dd61f9d42a48c022a183e7f4310a8a4ed7ae881edd136881ef6863282e2c.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i+x\\,j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=x\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y,z)=2\\,i.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246c2163-a431-53df-9c32-91cc7baa04f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253998} {:answer/id #uuid "664169a1-99e9-5129-a9ac-60cb4c0afd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254588} {:answer/id #uuid "66f7f9c9-6005-52af-8503-4204ea104780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254599} {:answer/id #uuid "ce110d77-de29-50d0-b4a3-b3cd16e56e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255501} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8e27bb-e7f4-5967-96b3-21b1a4efd7bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255506}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255506} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf119d84-49c7-595f-9a0e-210df016ca2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "db25e593-34c2-554c-af14-c5a728ea4219" :question/math-academy-id "q-122668" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f89653698d9c2bad0c2e6a77d4dad1953a9fd72f89cbfc08fd68aac802acd284.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $i$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=4\\,k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $y$-direction, so the $j$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=2\\,j\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $j$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=x\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y,z)=-j.$"}]}
